<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Upper and Lower Silesia</h1><p class="st-meta">1742 CE – 1918 CE · capital Opava</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The small part of Silesia that Austria kept after Frederick the Great's conquest, an industrial crown land of the Habsburgs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> seized most of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-breslau">Treaty of Breslau</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-berlin-1742">Peace of Berlin</a> in 1742 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> kept only a southern strip, including the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Troppau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krnov">Jägerndorf</a>. This remnant was governed as the Duchy of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, usually called Austrian Silesia.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Austrian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a> was administered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Troppau</a> (Opava), joined to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Moravia</a> from 1782 and from 1849 a separate crown land with its own diet. Its population was German, Czech and Polish, Catholic with Lutheran minorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> mining in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrava-karvina-coal-basin">Ostrava-Karviná</a> basin and the heavy industry around it made it one of the most industrialised parts of the monarchy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in 1918, the territory was claimed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. After a brief war over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a> in 1919, it was divided in 1920, with most going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-upper-and-lower-silesia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Upper and Lower Silesia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Urmas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Czech_Silesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1742 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Opava</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maria Theresa, Joseph II, Franz Joseph I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Czech, Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Austrian Silesia, Duchy of Silesia (Austrian)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1830&amp;focus=duchy-of-upper-and-lower-silesia" aria-label="Duchy of Upper and Lower Silesia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5090 440 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Upper and Lower Silesia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5410 696L5410 696M5410 696L5381 747M5410 696L5381 747M5410 696L5380 747M5410 696L5330 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5410 696h0M5381 747h0M5381 747h0M5380 747h0M5330 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5410 696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5410 696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Opava. <a href="/atlas?year=1830&amp;focus=duchy-of-upper-and-lower-silesia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
